[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"C# String Manipulation: A Comprehensive Guide"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Strings are fundamental data types in C#. Mastering string manipulation is crucial for any C# developer. This tutorial covers essential techniques for working with strings, including creation, modification, comparison, and formatting."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"String Creation and Immutability"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In C#, strings are immutable, meaning their values cannot be changed after creation. Any operation that appears to modify a string actually creates a new string object. Strings can be created using string literals or the 'String' constructor."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// String creation examples
string str1 = "Hello, World!";
string str2 = new string('C', 10); // Creates a string with 10 'C' characters
Console.WriteLine(str1);
Console.WriteLine(str2);"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic String Operations"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"C# provides various methods for performing common string operations. Some of the most frequently used methods include:"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'Length': Returns the number of characters in the string."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'Concat()': Concatenates two or more strings."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'Substring()': Extracts a portion of the string."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'IndexOf()': Finds the index of the first occurrence of a substring."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'LastIndexOf()': Finds the index of the last occurrence of a substring."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'Replace()': Replaces all occurrences of a substring with another string."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'ToUpper()': Converts the string to uppercase."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'ToLower()': Converts the string to lowercase."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'Trim()': Removes leading and trailing whitespace."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"'Split()': Splits the string into an array of substrings based on a delimiter."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"// String operations examples
string str = "  Hello, World!  ";
Console.WriteLine("Length: " + str.Length);
Console.WriteLine("Concat: " + string.Concat("C#", " Programming"));
Console.WriteLine("Substring: " + str.Substring(2, 5));
Console.WriteLine("IndexOf: " + str.IndexOf("World"));
Console.WriteLine("Replace: " + str.Replace("World", "C#"));
Console.WriteLine("ToUpper: " + str.ToUpper());
Console.WriteLine("ToLower: " + str.ToLower());
Console.WriteLine("Trim: " + str.Trim());
string[] words = str.Split(',');
foreach (string word in words)
{
    Console.WriteLine("Split: " + word.Trim());
}"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"String Comparison"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"C# provides several ways to compare strings. The '==' operator performs a case-sensitive ordinal comparison. For more control over the comparison, use the 'String.Compare()' or 'String.Equals()' methods."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"// String comparison examples
string strA = "Hello";
string strB = "hello";

Console.WriteLine("== comparison: " + (strA == strB)); // False
Console.WriteLine("Equals (Ordinal): " + string.Equals(strA, strB, StringComparison.Ordinal)); // False
Console.WriteLine("Equals (OrdinalIgnoreCase): " + string.Equals(strA, strB, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)); // True
Console.WriteLine("Compare (Ordinal): " + string.Compare(strA, strB, StringComparison.Ordinal)); // Returns a negative value
Console.WriteLine("Compare (OrdinalIgnoreCase): " + string.Compare(strA, strB, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)); // Returns 0"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"String Formatting"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"String formatting allows you to create strings with dynamic values. C# provides several ways to format strings, including composite formatting (using 'string.Format()') and string interpolation (using '$' prefix)."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"// String formatting examples
string name = "Alice";
int age = 30;

// Composite formatting
string formattedString1 = string.Format("Name: {0}, Age: {1}", name, age);
Console.WriteLine(formattedString1);

// String interpolation
string formattedString2 = $"Name: {name}, Age: {age}";
Console.WriteLine(formattedString2);"},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"StringBuilder Class"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"For scenarios involving frequent string modifications, the 'StringBuilder' class is more efficient than using regular strings. 'StringBuilder' is mutable, allowing you to modify its value without creating new string objects repeatedly."},{"order":27,"type":"code","text":"// StringBuilder example
using System.Text;

StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
sb.Append("Hello");
sb.Append(", ");
sb.Append("World!");
string result = sb.ToString();
Console.WriteLine(result);"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'StringBuilder' class provides methods like 'Append()', 'Insert()', 'Remove()', and 'Replace()' for modifying the string."},{"order":29,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial covered essential string manipulation techniques in C#. By understanding these concepts and methods, you can effectively work with strings in your C# applications. Remember to choose the appropriate method based on your specific needs, considering factors like performance and readability."}]
